    "dc.legislation.history" : [ {
      "value" : "<p>Legislative History: </p><p>The Right to Information Act, 2005</p><p></p><p>Short Title - The Right to Information Act, 2005</p><p>Act Number / Year - Act No. 22 of 2005</p><p>Act ID (India Code) - 200522 (Internal ID: 2005-22)</p><p>Introduced In - Lok Sabha (as The Right to Information Bill, 2004)</p><p>Presidential Assent Date - June 15, 2005</p><p>Official Gazette Publication - June 21, 2005</p><p>Enforcement Strategy - \tPhase 1 (Immediate provisions): June 15, 2005 (Ss. 4(1), 5(1)-(2), 12, 13, 15, 16, 24, 27, 28); Phase 2 (Full Enforcement): October 12, 2005 (120th day of enactment)</p><p>Nodal Ministry - Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions</p><p></p><p>Precedent &amp; Statutory Rationale</p><p></p><p>1.\tReplacement of the Weak 2002 Framework:</p><p>o\tThe preceding Freedom of Information Act, 2002 was widely criticized for containing excessive exemptions, weak enforcement mechanisms, and lacking proactive disclosure mandates. It was never notified into force.</p><p></p><p>2.\tPromotion of Transparency and Accountability:</p><p></p><p>o\tEnacted to provide a practical regime for citizens to secure access to information under the control of public authorities, thereby combating systemic corruption, promoting governmental transparency, and establishing independent appeal bodies. </p><p></p><p>Parliamentary Trajectory &amp; Enactment</p><p></p><p>•\tDecember 2004: The Right to Information Bill, 2004 was introduced in the Lok Sabha, reflecting wide inputs from the National Advisory Council (NAC) and civil society organizations.</p><p>•\tMay 2005: Passed by the Lok Sabha (May 11, 2005) and Rajya Sabha (May 12, 2005) with significant amendments strengthening penalty structures and disclosure rules.</p><p>•\tJune 15, 2005: Received Presidential Assent as Act No. 22 of 2005. </p><p>•\tOctober 12, 2005: Came into full operational effect across India upon the completion of the 120-day statutory transition window. </p><p></p><p>Core Statutory Framework Established</p><p></p><p>1.\tObligation of Public Authorities (Section 4): Mandates every public authority to maintain records duly cataloged and proactively publish vast categories of administrative data.</p><p>2.\tPublic Information Officers (Section 5): Requires public authorities to designate Central/State Public Information Officers (CPIOs/SPIOs) to process information requests within strict timeframes (typically 30 days).</p><p>3.\tInformation Commissions (Sections 12 &amp; 15): Establishes the autonomous Central Information Commission (CIC) and State Information Commissions (SICs) vested with powers of a civil court to penalize non-compliance and hear second appeals</p><p></p><p>Source: Official Records of Parliament, India Code, and Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>",
      "language" : null,
      "authority" : null,
      "confidence" : -1,
      "place" : 0
    } ],
